<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Indian Independence Act 1947</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1947">1947</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Indian Independence Act</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Indian Independence Act 1947 was an act of the British Parliament that partitioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> into the two independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">dominions</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> from 15 August 1947. It ended British rule and the British monarch's title of Emperor of India and freed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> from British paramountcy. It was passed in July 1947 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-attlee">Clement Attlee</a>'s Labour government.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>On 20 February 1947 the British prime minister, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clement-attlee">Clement Attlee</a>, announced that the British government would grant full self-government to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> by 30 June 1948 at the latest. Representatives of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-national-congress">Indian National Congress</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/all-india-muslim-league">Muslim League</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikh</a> community then reached agreement with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-mountbatten">Lord Mountbatten</a>, Viceroy and Governor-General of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, on what became known as the 3 June Plan or Mountbatten Plan. It accepted partition, gave the successor governments dominion status and the right to make their own constitutions, and allowed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> to join either India or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, with no option to remain independent.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The House of Commons voted for the bill on 15 July 1947, the House of Lords consented the next day, and the act received Royal Assent on 18 July. It divided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominion">Dominion</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and the Dominion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> from 15 August 1947 and partitioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bengal">Bengal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> between them, with boundary commissions to set the lines. Each dominion received a Governor-General as the Crown's representative, and each Constituent Assembly received complete legislative authority.</p><p>British suzerainty over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> lapsed, the office of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secretary-of-state-for-india">Secretary of State for India</a> was abolished, and the British monarch's title Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> was dropped, a change carried out by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/george-vi">George VI</a> by royal proclamation on 22 June 1948. The act also provided for dividing joint property and the armed forces.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Partition brought great violence. Many Muslims fled from what became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sikhism">Sikhs</a> from what became Pakistan to India, leaving behind their possessions. Mountbatten continued as the first Governor-General of India, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jawaharlal-nehru">Jawaharlal Nehru</a> as prime minister, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-ali-jinnah">Muhammad Ali Jinnah</a> became Governor-General of Pakistan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liaquat-ali-khan">Liaquat Ali Khan</a> its prime minister.</p><p>Over 550 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/princely-state">princely states</a> acceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> by 15 August, the exceptions being Junagadh, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jammu">Jammu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kashmir">Kashmir</a>. Maharaja Hari Singh of Kashmir, hesitating, faced a tribal invasion from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pakistan">Pakistan</a> and chose to accede to India, and the state has been disputed between the two since. Hyderabad was invaded by India in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-polo">Operation Polo</a> on 13 September 1948, and its Nizam signed the Instrument of Accession. Several Muslim-majority states joined Pakistan between October 1947 and March 1948, among them Bahawalpur, Swat and Kalat.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Indian Independence Act 1947” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Indian Independence Act</b> created the dominions of India and Pakistan on 14–15 August 1947.</blockquote><a href="/empire/british-raj">British Raj · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the <b>Indian Independence Act</b> of 1947, the British Raj was partitioned, and on 15 August 1947 the Dominion of India was created with Jawaharlal Nehru as prime minister.</blockquote><a href="/state/dominion-of-india">Dominion of India</a></li></ul></section>
